      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-1,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)={e}^{t}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=2\\sqrt{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{t}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
